[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Java Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Java Language Overview and Platform Evolution",
        "content": "# Java Language Overview and Platform Evolution\n\nJava is a general-purpose, class-based programming language used to build backend services, enterprise systems, automation tools, financial applications and cloud-hosted software. Its execution model separates source code from the operating system's native machine instructions. Developers write Java source code, compile it into bytecode, and execute that bytecode using a compatible Java Virtual Machine (JVM).\n\nJava is statically and strongly typed. Variables have declared types, and the compiler checks many invalid operations before an application starts. Java also provides object-oriented programming, exception handling, multithreading, networking libraries, collections, security facilities and automatic memory management.\n\nJava has evolved across many releases. Lambda expressions and streams introduced functional-style data processing. The module system added stronger application modularity. Records simplified immutable data-carrier classes. Pattern matching and other language improvements have reduced boilerplate and improved readability. A project must use language features supported by its configured Java release.\n\nExample:\n\n```java\npublic class HelloJava {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, Java!\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe class contains the program entry point. When launched, the JVM executes the `main` method and prints the message.\n\nJava's platform portability does not mean every Java application automatically runs everywhere. The target machine still needs a compatible runtime, required dependencies, supported operating-system features and correct configuration.\n\nIn DevOps work, Java knowledge helps teams select JDK versions, configure CI agents, compile and test applications, package JAR files, build container images and diagnose runtime failures. Record the Java version used by the build and runtime environments so that version differences do not create unexpected deployment problems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "JDK, JRE and JVM — Roles and Differences",
        "content": "# JDK, JRE and JVM — Roles and Differences\n\nThe JVM, JRE and JDK are related parts of the Java platform, but they have different responsibilities.\n\nThe Java Virtual Machine executes Java bytecode. It loads classes, verifies bytecode, manages runtime memory, executes threads and performs garbage collection. JVM implementations are specific to operating systems and processor architectures, even though Java bytecode is designed to be portable.\n\nThe Java Runtime Environment traditionally describes the JVM together with the libraries and supporting files required to run Java applications. A machine that only executes an already-built application needs runtime capabilities, not necessarily compiler tools. Modern Java distributions do not always provide a separate JRE download; teams often use a JDK distribution or create a reduced runtime image.\n\nThe Java Development Kit includes runtime capabilities and development tools. Important tools include `javac` for compilation, `java` for launching programs, `jar` for archive operations, and diagnostic tools such as `jcmd` and `jstack`.\n\nCheck the installed tools:\n\n```bash\njava -version\njavac -version\n```\n\n`java -version` reports the runtime launcher version. `javac -version` reports the compiler version. If the first command works but the second does not, the machine may lack compiler tools or its JDK `bin` directory may not be configured in PATH.\n\nTypical responsibilities:\n\n- JVM: executes bytecode and manages runtime behavior.\n- JRE: provides the environment required to run Java applications.\n- JDK: provides development, compilation and diagnostic tools alongside runtime capabilities.\n\nA CI build agent normally needs a JDK because it compiles and tests source code. A runtime container may use a smaller runtime-focused image if compilation has already completed. Keep the build and runtime versions compatible, verify the actual image contents and use controlled image versions rather than depending on an unpinned `latest` tag."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Java Source Code, Compilation and Bytecode",
        "content": "# Java Source Code, Compilation and Bytecode\n\nJava source code is normally stored in `.java` files. The Java compiler translates valid source code into bytecode stored in `.class` files. Bytecode is an instruction format understood by a compatible JVM rather than native instructions for one particular operating system.\n\nCreate `Greeting.java`:\n\n```java\npublic class Greeting {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String message = \"Build completed\";\n        System.out.println(message);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCompile the source:\n\n```bash\njavac Greeting.java\n```\n\nA successful compilation creates `Greeting.class`. Launch it with:\n\n```bash\njava Greeting\n```\n\nThe launcher locates the class, starts the JVM and invokes its `main` method. Supply the class name without the `.class` extension.\n\nCompilation checks syntax, type compatibility, access rules and other language constraints. A compilation error prevents the affected class from being generated. Successful compilation does not guarantee correct business behavior: runtime exceptions, incorrect logic, missing configuration and unavailable external services can still cause failures.\n\nCompiled output can be placed in a separate directory:\n\n```bash\njavac -d out Greeting.java\njava -cp out Greeting\n```\n\nThe `-d` option selects the output directory. The `-cp` option specifies the classpath used to locate classes and dependencies.\n\nJava applications are often packaged into JAR archives. A JAR can contain compiled classes, resources and metadata describing the entry point. Build tools such as Maven and Gradle automate compilation, dependency resolution, testing and packaging.\n\nIn CI/CD, compile and test using a known JDK, package the tested output and publish the resulting artifact. Promote the same artifact through environments instead of rebuilding different binaries for development, staging and production. This makes releases easier to reproduce and investigate."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Primitive Data Types and Reference Types",
        "content": "# Primitive Data Types and Reference Types\n\nJava defines eight primitive data types: `byte`, `short`, `int`, `long`, `float`, `double`, `char` and `boolean`.\n\nInteger types represent whole numbers with different ranges. `float` and `double` represent floating-point values. `char` represents a UTF-16 code unit, and `boolean` represents either `true` or `false`.\n\n```java\nint retryCount = 3;\nlong requestId = 9876543210L;\ndouble responseTime = 0.245;\nchar grade = 'A';\nboolean deploymentSuccessful = true;\n```\n\nThe `L` suffix identifies a long literal. Decimal literals are treated as `double` by default. Use an `f` suffix when assigning a literal to a `float`.\n\nReference types include classes, arrays, interfaces, enums and records. A reference variable refers to an object rather than containing the complete object value. A reference can also contain `null`, meaning it currently refers to no object.\n\n```java\nString serviceName = \"payment-api\";\nString anotherName = serviceName;\n\nint[] ports = {8080, 8081};\n```\n\nAssigning `serviceName` to `anotherName` does not create a new String object. Both variables refer to the same object. This distinction is especially important with mutable objects because changes made through one reference may be visible through another.\n\nPrimitive variables cannot be assigned `null`. Reference variables can. Java provides wrapper classes such as `Integer`, `Long`, `Double` and `Boolean` when an object representation is needed. Collections store objects, so developers commonly use types such as `List<Integer>` instead of a primitive collection declaration.\n\nChoose data types according to the meaning and valid range of the data. For financial calculations requiring decimal precision, `BigDecimal` is often more appropriate than binary floating-point types. Understand reference sharing when debugging unexpected state changes and designing application models."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Variables, Scope and Type Conversion",
        "content": "# Variables, Scope and Type Conversion\n\nA variable is a named location associated with a data type. Java has local variables, instance variables and static variables.\n\nA local variable is declared inside a method, constructor or block and is accessible only within that scope. An instance variable belongs to an object, so separate objects have separate instance state. A static variable belongs to the class and is shared by its instances.\n\n```java\npublic class DeploymentConfig {\n    static int defaultPort = 8080;\n    String environment;\n\n    public void start() {\n        int retryLimit = 3;\n        System.out.println(retryLimit);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nLocal variables must be definitely assigned before they are read. Instance and static fields receive default values during initialization. A variable declared inside a block cannot be accessed after that block ends.\n\nJava supports widening conversions such as `int` to `long`:\n\n```java\nint attempts = 5;\nlong totalAttempts = attempts;\n```\n\nNarrowing conversions may lose information and require an explicit cast:\n\n```java\ndouble percentage = 98.75;\nint wholePercentage = (int) percentage;\n```\n\nThe result is `98` because the cast truncates the fractional part; it does not round the value.\n\nText can be converted into numbers using parsing methods:\n\n```java\nString portText = \"8080\";\nint port = Integer.parseInt(portText);\n```\n\nInvalid input causes a `NumberFormatException`. Validate data received from environment variables, files and API requests before using it.\n\nVariable names should explain their purpose. Prefer `connectionTimeoutSeconds` over unclear names such as `x`. In production applications, central configuration binding and validation reduce scattered conversions and make configuration errors easier to diagnose."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Operators and Expression Evaluation",
        "content": "# Operators and Expression Evaluation\n\nJava operators perform calculations, comparisons, logical checks and assignments. Arithmetic operators include `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `%`. Comparison operators include `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>` and their inclusive forms. Logical operators include `&&`, `||` and `!`.\n\n```java\nint totalRequests = 120;\nint failedRequests = 6;\n\ndouble failureRate =\n    (double) failedRequests / totalRequests * 100;\n\nboolean alertRequired = failureRate > 5.0;\n\nSystem.out.println(failureRate);\nSystem.out.println(alertRequired);\n```\n\nThe cast to `double` prevents integer division from discarding the fractional part. Without the cast, dividing two integers would produce an integer result before multiplication.\n\nJava follows operator precedence rules. Multiplication and division are evaluated before addition and subtraction unless parentheses change the order. Use parentheses when they make a business calculation clearer.\n\nThe `&&` and `||` operators use short-circuit evaluation. With `&&`, the right side is evaluated only if the left side is true. With `||`, the right side is evaluated only if the left side is false.\n\n```java\nString environment = System.getenv(\"APP_ENV\");\n\nif (environment != null && environment.equals(\"production\")) {\n    System.out.println(\"Production configuration selected\");\n}\n```\n\nThe null check prevents the second condition from dereferencing a null value.\n\nFor objects such as strings, use `equals()` to compare content. The `==` operator compares reference identity for objects.\n\nWatch for integer overflow, integer division, floating-point precision and expressions with unclear precedence. Keep complicated calculations readable, use named intermediate variables and test boundary values such as zero, negative numbers and maximum expected counts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Conditional Statements and Control Flow",
        "content": "# Conditional Statements and Control Flow\n\nControl flow determines which statements execute. Java uses `if`, `else if` and `else` for conditional decisions. An `if` condition must evaluate to a boolean value.\n\n```java\nint httpStatus = 503;\n\nif (httpStatus >= 200 && httpStatus < 300) {\n    System.out.println(\"Request succeeded\");\n} else if (httpStatus >= 500) {\n    System.out.println(\"Server-side failure\");\n} else if (httpStatus >= 400) {\n    System.out.println(\"Client-side failure\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Other response\");\n}\n```\n\nThe order of conditions matters. A broad condition placed before a more specific condition may prevent the later branch from executing. Keep conditions understandable and separate complex business rules into helper methods.\n\nA switch statement is useful when one expression is compared against several alternatives. Modern Java releases support switch expressions:\n\n```java\nString severity = switch (httpStatus / 100) {\n    case 2 -> \"SUCCESS\";\n    case 4 -> \"CLIENT_ERROR\";\n    case 5 -> \"SERVER_ERROR\";\n    default -> \"OTHER\";\n};\n```\n\nUse syntax supported by the Java release configured in the project. Traditional switch statements can fall through when `break` is omitted. Arrow-style switch rules avoid that traditional fall-through behavior.\n\nControl flow is central to application decisions: choosing an environment, classifying HTTP responses, validating input or determining whether a deployment should continue. Test every meaningful branch, including unexpected and boundary values. A condition that is never tested can hide defects until production traffic reaches it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Loops, Branching and Iteration",
        "content": "# Loops, Branching and Iteration\n\nLoops repeat a block of code while a condition remains satisfied or while elements remain in a sequence. Java provides `for`, enhanced `for`, `while` and `do-while` loops.\n\nA traditional `for` loop is useful when the iteration count or index is important:\n\n```java\nfor (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 3; attempt++) {\n    System.out.println(\"Attempt \" + attempt);\n}\n```\n\nAn enhanced `for` loop iterates over arrays and iterable collections without requiring manual index management:\n\n```java\nString[] environments = {\"dev\", \"test\", \"prod\"};\n\nfor (String environment : environments) {\n    System.out.println(environment);\n}\n```\n\nA `while` loop checks its condition before executing the body. A `do-while` loop executes the body at least once because its condition is checked afterward.\n\n```java\nint remaining = 3;\n\nwhile (remaining > 0) {\n    System.out.println(\"Remaining: \" + remaining);\n    remaining--;\n}\n```\n\nThe `break` statement exits the nearest loop. The `continue` statement skips the remainder of the current iteration and proceeds to the next one. Use them carefully so the loop's termination and processing behavior remain clear.\n\nAn accidental infinite loop can consume CPU and prevent a service from completing work. Always verify that loop conditions can eventually become false. When processing external collections or paginated API results, account for empty responses, repeated pages and changing data.\n\nIn operational code, retry loops should have bounded attempts, appropriate delays and clear failure handling. Do not create an unbounded retry loop around a failing network operation. Use established retry policies with timeouts and backoff when working with distributed services."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Classes, Objects and Methods",
        "content": "# Classes, Objects and Methods\n\nA class defines the structure and behavior of objects. It can declare fields for state, constructors for initialization and methods for operations. An object is a runtime instance created from a class.\n\n```java\npublic class Service {\n    private String name;\n\n    public Service(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n\n    public void start() {\n        System.out.println(name + \" started\");\n    }\n\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCreate and use an object:\n\n```java\nService paymentService = new Service(\"payment-api\");\npaymentService.start();\n```\n\nThe `new` operator creates an object and invokes its constructor. The variable `paymentService` stores a reference to that object.\n\nA method defines an operation. Its signature includes the method name and parameter types. A method may return a value or use `void` when it returns no result. Parameters allow callers to provide input, while return values allow methods to provide results.\n\n```java\npublic int calculateTotal(int unitPrice, int quantity) {\n    return unitPrice * quantity;\n}\n```\n\nThe `private` modifier restricts direct access to the field from outside its class. Public methods provide controlled interaction with the object's state.\n\nStatic methods belong to the class rather than a particular object. Instance methods operate in the context of an object and can access its instance state.\n\nKeep classes focused on a clear responsibility. Avoid classes that combine unrelated operations such as HTTP handling, database access, configuration parsing and business rules. Smaller, cohesive classes are easier to test, maintain and troubleshoot."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "Packages, Imports and Access Modifiers",
        "content": "# Packages, Imports and Access Modifiers\n\nPackages organize related Java types into namespaces. They help prevent naming conflicts and provide a structure for large applications. A package declaration normally appears at the beginning of a Java source file.\n\n```java\npackage com.example.payment;\n\npublic class PaymentService {\n}\n```\n\nThe package name usually corresponds to a directory structure under the source root:\n\n```text\nsrc/\n  main/\n    java/\n      com/\n        example/\n          payment/\n            PaymentService.java\n```\n\nThe `import` statement allows a type to be referred to by its simple name instead of its fully qualified name.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\n\nList<String> services = new ArrayList<>();\n```\n\nJava provides access modifiers that control where classes and members can be accessed. `public` allows access wherever the declaring type is accessible. `private` restricts access to the declaring class. `protected` allows access within the package and through subclass access rules. A member with no explicit modifier has package-private access.\n\nTop-level classes can be public or package-private. Only one public top-level class is normally declared in a source file, and the filename must match that public class name.\n\nUse packages to reflect application responsibilities, such as controllers, services, repositories, domain models and configuration. Avoid placing every class in one package or creating excessively deep package structures without a clear reason.\n\nAccess modifiers support encapsulation. Keep implementation details private and expose only the operations that other parts of the application genuinely need. This reduces accidental coupling and makes refactoring safer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Strings, Wrapper Classes and Autoboxing",
        "content": "# Strings, Wrapper Classes and Autoboxing\n\nThe `String` class represents text. Strings are immutable: once a String object has been created, its character content cannot be changed. Operations such as concatenation produce a new String value rather than modifying the original object.\n\n```java\nString service = \"payment\";\nString fullName = service + \"-api\";\n\nSystem.out.println(fullName);\n```\n\nUse `equals()` for content comparison:\n\n```java\nString environment = \"production\";\n\nif (\"production\".equals(environment)) {\n    System.out.println(\"Production mode\");\n}\n```\n\nPlacing the known non-null literal first avoids a null dereference if `environment` is null.\n\nFor repeated text construction inside loops, `StringBuilder` is usually more efficient than repeatedly creating intermediate strings:\n\n```java\nStringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n    result.append(\"service-\").append(i).append(\" \");\n}\n\nSystem.out.println(result);\n```\n\nWrapper classes represent primitive values as objects. Examples include `Integer` for `int`, `Long` for `long`, `Double` for `double` and `Boolean` for `boolean`.\n\nAutoboxing converts a primitive into its wrapper representation. Unboxing extracts the primitive value:\n\n```java\nint count = 10;\nInteger boxedCount = count;\n\nint actualCount = boxedCount;\n```\n\nUnboxing a null wrapper reference causes a `NullPointerException`. Validate nullable values before using them as primitives.\n\nWrapper types are necessary in generic collections such as `List<Integer>`. Be aware that wrapper objects can introduce nullability and additional allocation considerations. Use the appropriate type for the application model, compare values correctly and avoid unnecessary conversions in performance-sensitive code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Arrays and Multidimensional Arrays",
        "content": "# Arrays and Multidimensional Arrays\n\nAn array stores a fixed number of elements of the same component type. Its length is determined when the array is created and cannot be changed afterward. Array indexing starts at zero, and the final valid index is `length - 1`.\n\n```java\nint[] ports = {8080, 8081, 9090};\n\nSystem.out.println(ports[0]);\nSystem.out.println(ports.length);\n```\n\nAn array can also be created with a specified size:\n\n```java\nString[] services = new String[3];\nservices[0] = \"api\";\nservices[1] = \"worker\";\nservices[2] = \"scheduler\";\n```\n\nElements receive default values when an array is created. Numeric elements default to zero, boolean elements to false and reference elements to null.\n\nAccessing an invalid index causes `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Check the array length before using an index, especially when the index comes from external input.\n\nA multidimensional array is an array whose elements are themselves arrays:\n\n```java\nint[][] deploymentMatrix = {\n    {1, 0, 1},\n    {1, 1, 0}\n};\n\nSystem.out.println(deploymentMatrix[1][0]);\n```\n\nJava supports arrays with rows of different lengths because each row is a separate array. This is sometimes called a jagged array.\n\nArrays are useful when the size is fixed and indexed access is required. When the number of elements changes dynamically, collections such as `ArrayList` are often more suitable.\n\nArrays are objects and can be passed to methods by passing their references. A method that changes an array element can affect the same array observed by the caller. Consider this shared mutable state when designing methods and tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-13",
        "title": "Java Development and Execution Workflow",
        "content": "# Java Development and Execution Workflow\n\nA Java development workflow moves source code through editing, compilation, testing, packaging and execution. A developer typically writes source files under a source directory, declares dependencies, compiles the code and runs automated tests before packaging an application.\n\nA simple command-line workflow is:\n\n```bash\njavac -d out src/HelloJava.java\njava -cp out HelloJava\n```\n\nReal applications usually have multiple classes and external libraries. Build tools such as Maven and Gradle provide dependency management, standard directory layouts, build lifecycles, test execution and artifact packaging.\n\nA typical Maven workflow includes:\n\n```bash\nmvn clean\nmvn test\nmvn package\n```\n\n`clean` removes generated build output. `test` compiles and executes tests. `package` creates the configured distributable artifact after the required earlier lifecycle phases.\n\nA CI pipeline should use a controlled JDK version, restore dependencies from approved repositories, compile the source, execute automated tests, inspect test results and publish the packaged artifact. Build failures should stop promotion rather than silently allowing an unverified artifact to reach production.\n\nThe runtime environment then launches the packaged application using a compatible Java runtime. Configuration such as ports, database addresses and credentials should be supplied through controlled environment-specific configuration rather than hardcoded into source code.\n\nKeep build logs, test reports, artifact checksums and version information. These records help identify which source revision and build produced a deployed application. Reproducible build inputs and immutable artifacts make release investigation and rollback more reliable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-14",
        "title": "Java Language Troubleshooting and Best Practices",
        "content": "# Java Language Troubleshooting and Best Practices\n\nJava failures can occur during compilation, application startup or runtime execution. Identify the failure stage before changing code or configuration.\n\nCompilation errors often include invalid syntax, missing imports, incompatible types, inaccessible members or unsupported language features. Check the compiler output and confirm that the configured JDK supports the source version used by the project.\n\nRuntime exceptions occur after the application starts executing. Common examples include `NullPointerException`, `NumberFormatException`, `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` and `ClassCastException`. Read the exception type, message and stack trace. The first application-owned stack frame often points toward the code that needs investigation.\n\nA missing class at runtime may produce `ClassNotFoundException` or `NoClassDefFoundError`. Check whether the required dependency is included in the packaged artifact and available through the runtime classpath or module path.\n\nUseful checks include:\n\n```bash\njava -version\njavac -version\njar --list --file application.jar\n```\n\nFor a packaged application, inspect the archive contents and verify that the required classes and resources are present. Compare the Java version, startup command, environment variables and dependency versions between a working environment and the failing environment.\n\nFollow these practices:\n\n- Use meaningful class, method and variable names.\n- Keep methods focused and avoid unnecessary complexity.\n- Validate external input and configuration.\n- Prefer explicit types and clear control flow.\n- Use `equals()` for object content comparison.\n- Handle exceptions at appropriate application boundaries.\n- Avoid swallowing exceptions without logging or recovery.\n- Write tests for normal behavior, invalid input and boundary conditions.\n- Keep Java versions and dependencies controlled in CI/CD.\n- Never hardcode production credentials in source code.\n\nA useful troubleshooting process reproduces the problem, captures the exact error, identifies the failing layer, verifies assumptions and tests the correction. Avoid changing multiple unrelated settings at once because that makes the actual cause harder to identify."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Java Project Structure",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Establish the language or platform fundamentals required to understand this subject. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace one small example from its initial input through the relevant language or framework operations to its observable result.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain the internal concepts that determine how this subject behaves. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a concrete example to show the sequence of internal operations and identify the state before and after each important operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Architecture and Component Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Architecture and Component Responsibilities\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Describe the architecture and the responsibility of each major component. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Identify the main components, their boundaries, the information exchanged between them, and which component owns validation, execution, state, configuration, or error reporting. Explain how dependencies should flow without creating unnecessary coupling.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nDraw or document the component interaction for a realistic application and identify the responsibility of each component.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nIdentify the main components, their boundaries, the information exchanged between them, and which component owns validation, execution, state, configuration, or error reporting. Explain how dependencies should flow without creating unnecessary coupling.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Execution Lifecycle",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Execution Lifecycle\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Follow the complete lifecycle from initialization to completion. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain initialization, configuration loading, dependency discovery, execution, resource use, completion, and cleanup where applicable. Include the order of operations and explain what happens when an earlier lifecycle step fails.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace a representative operation in chronological order and identify the logs, runtime state, or outputs that prove each stage completed.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain initialization, configuration loading, dependency discovery, execution, resource use, completion, and cleanup where applicable. Include the order of operations and explain what happens when an earlier lifecycle step fails.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Configuration and Implementation",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Configuration and Implementation\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Show how this subject is configured and implemented correctly. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant configuration choices, their accepted values, defaults, validation requirements, and impact on runtime behavior. Separate source-controlled settings from environment-specific values and sensitive information.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nImplement a small, realistic configuration example and verify the effective result rather than assuming that a saved configuration was loaded.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant configuration choices, their accepted values, defaults, validation requirements, and impact on runtime behavior. Separate source-controlled settings from environment-specific values and sensitive information.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Code Structure and Design",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Code Structure and Design\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain how to organize implementation code for maintainability. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe suitable classes, interfaces, packages, methods, resource files, and boundaries for this subject. Explain how to avoid excessive responsibilities, duplicated logic, hidden dependencies, and unnecessary coupling.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nShow how a feature should be divided into focused components and explain which component owns each operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe suitable classes, interfaces, packages, methods, resource files, and boundaries for this subject. Explain how to avoid excessive responsibilities, duplicated logic, hidden dependencies, and unnecessary coupling.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Practical Development Workflow",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Practical Development Workflow\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Java project structure separates application source code, test code, resources, build configuration, generated artifacts, and documentation. This separation makes ownership, compilation, testing, packaging, and maintenance predictable.\n\nThis section focuses on Describe the everyday development workflow used to implement and maintain this subject. The explanation is specific to **Java Project Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nBuild tools identify conventional source roots, resolve dependencies, apply compiler settings, copy resources, execute tests, and package output. Maven commonly uses src/main/java, src/main/resources, src/test/java, and src/test/resources, with target holding generated build output.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Cover repository changes, implementation order, local configuration, build commands, test execution, code review, and artifact handling. Explain how a developer can reproduce the same result on another machine or CI agent.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production repository can organize code into API, service, domain, repository, configuration, and exception packages. Unit tests mirror important application packages, while application properties and logging configuration remain under the resource directory.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse a Maven layout with src/main/java for production classes, src/main/resources for runtime resources, src/test/java for automated tests, and pom.xml for build configuration. Verify the layout with mvn clean test and inspect the generated target directory.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nComplete one small change from source modification through build and verification, recording the commands and expected outputs.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include placing Java classes outside configured source roots, committing generated target files, mixing test-only dependencies into production scope, creating circular package dependencies, and storing credentials directly in configuration files.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nCover repository changes, implementation order, local configuration, build commands, test execution, code review, and artifact handling. Explain how a developer can reproduce the same result on another machine or CI agent.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
