  {
    "topicTitle": "Spring Boot Application Structure",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Establish the language or platform fundamentals required to understand this subject. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace one small example from its initial input through the relevant language or framework operations to its observable result.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain the internal concepts that determine how this subject behaves. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a concrete example to show the sequence of internal operations and identify the state before and after each important operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Architecture and Component Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Architecture and Component Responsibilities\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Describe the architecture and the responsibility of each major component. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Identify the main components, their boundaries, the information exchanged between them, and which component owns validation, execution, state, configuration, or error reporting. Explain how dependencies should flow without creating unnecessary coupling.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nDraw or document the component interaction for a realistic application and identify the responsibility of each component.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nIdentify the main components, their boundaries, the information exchanged between them, and which component owns validation, execution, state, configuration, or error reporting. Explain how dependencies should flow without creating unnecessary coupling.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-4",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Execution Lifecycle",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Execution Lifecycle\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Follow the complete lifecycle from initialization to completion. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain initialization, configuration loading, dependency discovery, execution, resource use, completion, and cleanup where applicable. Include the order of operations and explain what happens when an earlier lifecycle step fails.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace a representative operation in chronological order and identify the logs, runtime state, or outputs that prove each stage completed.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain initialization, configuration loading, dependency discovery, execution, resource use, completion, and cleanup where applicable. Include the order of operations and explain what happens when an earlier lifecycle step fails.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-5",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Configuration and Implementation",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Configuration and Implementation\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Show how this subject is configured and implemented correctly. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant configuration choices, their accepted values, defaults, validation requirements, and impact on runtime behavior. Separate source-controlled settings from environment-specific values and sensitive information.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nImplement a small, realistic configuration example and verify the effective result rather than assuming that a saved configuration was loaded.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant configuration choices, their accepted values, defaults, validation requirements, and impact on runtime behavior. Separate source-controlled settings from environment-specific values and sensitive information.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-6",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Code Structure and Design",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Code Structure and Design\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain how to organize implementation code for maintainability. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe suitable classes, interfaces, packages, methods, resource files, and boundaries for this subject. Explain how to avoid excessive responsibilities, duplicated logic, hidden dependencies, and unnecessary coupling.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nShow how a feature should be divided into focused components and explain which component owns each operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe suitable classes, interfaces, packages, methods, resource files, and boundaries for this subject. Explain how to avoid excessive responsibilities, duplicated logic, hidden dependencies, and unnecessary coupling.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-7",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Practical Development Workflow",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Practical Development Workflow\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Describe the everyday development workflow used to implement and maintain this subject. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Cover repository changes, implementation order, local configuration, build commands, test execution, code review, and artifact handling. Explain how a developer can reproduce the same result on another machine or CI agent.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nComplete one small change from source modification through build and verification, recording the commands and expected outputs.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nCover repository changes, implementation order, local configuration, build commands, test execution, code review, and artifact handling. Explain how a developer can reproduce the same result on another machine or CI agent.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-8",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Testing and Verification",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Testing and Verification\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Define how to verify correctness at multiple levels. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Distinguish unit tests, integration tests, contract checks, static validation, and end-to-end tests where relevant. Explain test inputs, expected outcomes, failure assertions, isolation, and how test results should be used as release evidence.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nCreate a positive test, a negative test, and a boundary-condition test. Run them and explain what each result proves.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDistinguish unit tests, integration tests, contract checks, static validation, and end-to-end tests where relevant. Explain test inputs, expected outcomes, failure assertions, isolation, and how test results should be used as release evidence.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-9",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Common Errors and Failure Modes",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Common Errors and Failure Modes\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Identify realistic failures and explain their underlying causes. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: For each failure, connect the visible symptom to the likely technical cause. Separate configuration mistakes, programming defects, dependency failures, environment differences, resource exhaustion, and permission problems.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nReproduce one controlled failure, capture its error message, correct the root cause, and verify that the original operation succeeds.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nFor each failure, connect the visible symptom to the likely technical cause. Separate configuration mistakes, programming defects, dependency failures, environment differences, resource exhaustion, and permission problems.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-10",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Debugging and Diagnostics",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Debugging and Diagnostics\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Provide a systematic method for diagnosing failures. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Start with the exact symptom, timestamp, affected component, and recent change. Explain which logs, stack traces, runtime values, build reports, process details, or network observations can confirm or eliminate each hypothesis.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a hypothesis-driven investigation: collect evidence, run one targeted check at a time, record the result, and verify the fix with the original failing operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nStart with the exact symptom, timestamp, affected component, and recent change. Explain which logs, stack traces, runtime values, build reports, process details, or network observations can confirm or eliminate each hypothesis.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-11",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Performance and Optimization",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Performance and Optimization\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain the performance characteristics and optimization opportunities. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Identify relevant cost factors such as CPU work, memory allocation, I/O, network calls, locking, collection lookup, startup overhead, or dependency initialization. Explain how to measure the bottleneck before changing implementation.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nEstablish a baseline, change one performance factor, repeat the same workload, and compare latency, throughput, resource consumption, and correctness.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nIdentify relevant cost factors such as CPU work, memory allocation, I/O, network calls, locking, collection lookup, startup overhead, or dependency initialization. Explain how to measure the bottleneck before changing implementation.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-10-section-12",
        "title": "Spring Boot Application Structure — Security Considerations",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Application Structure — Security Considerations\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nA Spring Boot application structure organizes entry points, controllers, services, repositories, domain models, configuration, exception handling, and resources into understandable responsibilities.\n\nThis section focuses on Identify security boundaries and controls relevant to this subject. The explanation is specific to **Spring Boot Application Structure** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nComponent scanning discovers eligible beans from configured package roots. The application context manages bean creation and dependencies, while Spring MVC routes HTTP requests through controller mappings and message converters.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain input validation, trust boundaries, access control, secret handling, dependency risk, least privilege, secure defaults, and sensitive-data exposure where applicable. Describe how insecure configuration or implementation can be exploited.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment-management API can place HTTP request handling in controllers, validation and workflow logic in services, persistence operations in repositories, and deployment-specific settings in configuration classes.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nSeparate controller, service, repository, domain, configuration, and exception-handling responsibilities. Use constructor injection and package boundaries that keep HTTP concerns out of business logic and persistence concerns out of controllers.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nReview the implementation for one realistic security weakness, apply an appropriate control, and verify that unauthorized or malformed operations are rejected.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nTypical issues include placing the main application class in the wrong package, creating oversized controllers, mixing persistence logic with HTTP handling, accidental component-scan gaps, and introducing circular bean dependencies.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain input validation, trust boundaries, access control, secret handling, dependency risk, least privilege, secure defaults, and sensitive-data exposure where applicable. Describe how insecure configuration or implementation can be exploited.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Dependency Injection",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-1",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Establish the language or platform fundamentals required to understand this subject. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace one small example from its initial input through the relevant language or framework operations to its observable result.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-2",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain the internal concepts that determine how this subject behaves. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a concrete example to show the sequence of internal operations and identify the state before and after each important operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-3",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Architecture and Component Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Architecture and Component Responsibilities\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Describe the architecture and the responsibility of each major component. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Identify the main components, their boundaries, the information exchanged between them, and which component owns validation, execution, state, configuration, or error reporting. Explain how dependencies should flow without creating unnecessary coupling.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nDraw or document the component interaction for a realistic application and identify the responsibility of each component.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nIdentify the main components, their boundaries, the information exchanged between them, and which component owns validation, execution, state, configuration, or error reporting. Explain how dependencies should flow without creating unnecessary coupling.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-4",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Execution Lifecycle",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Execution Lifecycle\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Follow the complete lifecycle from initialization to completion. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain initialization, configuration loading, dependency discovery, execution, resource use, completion, and cleanup where applicable. Include the order of operations and explain what happens when an earlier lifecycle step fails.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace a representative operation in chronological order and identify the logs, runtime state, or outputs that prove each stage completed.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain initialization, configuration loading, dependency discovery, execution, resource use, completion, and cleanup where applicable. Include the order of operations and explain what happens when an earlier lifecycle step fails.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-5",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Configuration and Implementation",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Configuration and Implementation\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Show how this subject is configured and implemented correctly. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant configuration choices, their accepted values, defaults, validation requirements, and impact on runtime behavior. Separate source-controlled settings from environment-specific values and sensitive information.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nImplement a small, realistic configuration example and verify the effective result rather than assuming that a saved configuration was loaded.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant configuration choices, their accepted values, defaults, validation requirements, and impact on runtime behavior. Separate source-controlled settings from environment-specific values and sensitive information.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Code Structure and Design",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Code Structure and Design\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain how to organize implementation code for maintainability. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe suitable classes, interfaces, packages, methods, resource files, and boundaries for this subject. Explain how to avoid excessive responsibilities, duplicated logic, hidden dependencies, and unnecessary coupling.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nShow how a feature should be divided into focused components and explain which component owns each operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe suitable classes, interfaces, packages, methods, resource files, and boundaries for this subject. Explain how to avoid excessive responsibilities, duplicated logic, hidden dependencies, and unnecessary coupling.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-11-section-7",
        "title": "Dependency Injection — Practical Development Workflow",
        "content": "# Dependency Injection — Practical Development Workflow\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nDependency injection is a design technique in which an object receives the collaborators it needs instead of constructing those collaborators internally. Spring manages this process through its application context.\n\nThis section focuses on Describe the everyday development workflow used to implement and maintain this subject. The explanation is specific to **Dependency Injection** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nSpring identifies bean definitions, resolves dependency types and qualifiers, determines bean scope, creates instances, and performs injection. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit and allows objects to be constructed in a valid state.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Cover repository changes, implementation order, local configuration, build commands, test execution, code review, and artifact handling. Explain how a developer can reproduce the same result on another machine or CI agent.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA DeploymentService can receive a DeploymentRepository and a NotificationClient through its constructor. Unit tests can provide mock implementations, while production configuration supplies database-backed and network-backed implementations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nDefine a service interface and inject its implementation through a constructor. Use a test configuration or mock dependency to verify the service independently, and inspect Spring startup diagnostics when bean creation or dependency resolution fails.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nComplete one small change from source modification through build and verification, recording the commands and expected outputs.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nProblems include circular dependencies, ambiguous beans, excessive field injection, hidden optional dependencies, incorrect bean scopes, and service classes that depend on too many unrelated collaborators.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nCover repository changes, implementation order, local configuration, build commands, test execution, code review, and artifact handling. Explain how a developer can reproduce the same result on another machine or CI agent.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
