        "id": "stage-1-topic-15-section-10",
        "title": "Logging and Actuator — Debugging and Diagnostics",
        "content": "# Logging and Actuator — Debugging and Diagnostics\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nLogging records application events for debugging, operations, auditing, and performance analysis. Spring Boot Actuator exposes operational endpoints that help inspect application health, metrics, and runtime information.\n\nThis section focuses on Provide a systematic method for diagnosing failures. The explanation is specific to **Logging and Actuator** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nLogging frameworks process levels, logger hierarchies, appenders, layouts, and structured fields. Actuator integrates management endpoints with the application context and can expose health indicators, metrics, environment information, and application mappings subject to configuration.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Start with the exact symptom, timestamp, affected component, and recent change. Explain which logs, stack traces, runtime values, build reports, process details, or network observations can confirm or eliminate each hypothesis.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA production service can emit structured logs containing request identifiers and deployment versions, expose a restricted health endpoint for Kubernetes probes, and publish metrics for Prometheus to collect.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse parameterized structured logging with useful identifiers and avoid logging credentials or personal data. Expose only required Actuator endpoints, restrict management access, and verify health and metrics from the intended monitoring network.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a hypothesis-driven investigation: collect evidence, run one targeted check at a time, record the result, and verify the fix with the original failing operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nOperational risks include logging passwords or tokens, enabling sensitive Actuator endpoints publicly, using excessive debug logging, omitting correlation identifiers, and treating a basic process health response as proof that all dependencies are healthy.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nStart with the exact symptom, timestamp, affected component, and recent change. Explain which logs, stack traces, runtime values, build reports, process details, or network observations can confirm or eliminate each hypothesis.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
